window.AGREEMENTS = {
  participation: {"title":"The Volunteer and Participation Agreement","version":"vpa-2026-07-v1","html":"<p class=\"t-small\"><strong>THE UPSKILLING LABS</strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">Volunteer Participation Agreement</p><p class=\"t-small\"><em>A policy acknowledgment for all volunteers and participants</em></p><p class=\"t-small\">The Upskilling Labs, Inc. (\"The Labs\") is a community-driven organization supporting people navigating career transitions in a changing labor market. We do this through peer-driven learning cycles and open, public-facing projects. Volunteers are essential to making it work.</p><p class=\"t-small\">By signing this Volunteer Participation Agreement (\"Agreement\"), you confirm that you have read, understood, and agree to the terms and expectations set out below. This document is a policy acknowledgment, not a contract of employment or a bilateral contract. It establishes the terms under which The Labs welcomes your participation.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Volunteers contribute across a range of functions, including facilitation, strategy, technology, content, design, and community support.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">1. What You Gain as a Volunteer</div><p class=\"t-small\">Volunteering with The Labs is an exchange. While you contribute your time and skills without financial compensation, The Labs is committed to making your participation genuinely valuable. By volunteering, you can expect:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\"><strong>Learning and Skill Development.</strong> Access to real-world practice in facilitation, content development, technology, community building, and other applied skills. You will work on actual projects with tangible outcomes, not simulated exercises.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\"><strong>Networking and Community.</strong> Connection to a growing network of professionals, practitioners, and community members. Labs events, cohorts, and projects are designed to create meaningful peer relationships.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\"><strong>Volunteer Experience and Recognition.</strong> Verified volunteer experience you can reference on your resume and professional profiles, including LinkedIn. With permission, The Labs may publicly acknowledge your contributions across its platforms.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\"><strong>Portfolio and Published Work.</strong> Many volunteer contributions result in open-source resources that are publicly available and attributable. Content contributors, R&amp;D volunteers, and workshop facilitators may be credited on published materials, giving you shareable, public-facing proof of your work.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\"><strong>Access to Labs Programs and Resources.</strong> Participation in Labs programming, workshops, and learning cycles that are otherwise available to enrolled participants. Volunteers are embedded in the community, not separate from it.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\"><strong>Mission-Driven Impact.</strong> The direct experience of contributing to something that matters. The Labs works at the intersection of workforce development, community resilience, and open knowledge — your work here has real consequences for real people navigating career transitions.</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\">The sections below set out the mutual expectations that make this exchange work well for everyone.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">2. Your Status as a Volunteer</div><p class=\"t-small\">By participating, you acknowledge that:</p><p class=\"t-small\">a)  You are volunteering your time freely and are not an employee, &gt; contractor, partner, agent, or representative of The Labs.</p><p class=\"t-small\">b)  You will not receive compensation or benefits for your volunteer &gt; activities.</p><p class=\"t-small\">c)  Any expense reimbursement must be pre-approved in writing by The &gt; Labs before it is incurred.</p><p class=\"t-small\">d)  You have received and reviewed The Labs' onboarding materials and &gt; understand the expectations applicable to your role.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">3. Participation and Conduct</div><p class=\"t-small\">While participating as a volunteer, you agree to:</p><p class=\"t-small\">a)  Engage in a manner consistent with The Labs' community standards, &gt; onboarding materials, and any applicable code of conduct.</p><p class=\"t-small\">b)  Treat all participants, staff, and fellow volunteers with respect, &gt; and avoid harassment, discrimination, or retaliation of any kind.</p><p class=\"t-small\">c)  Not use The Labs' community, events, or platforms to directly sell &gt; your services or paid offerings without written consent from The &gt; Labs. Reasonable professional networking is welcome; high-pressure &gt; sales behavior is not.</p><p class=\"t-small\">d)  Exercise reasonable care when providing guidance, feedback, &gt; facilitation, or technical support, and avoid representing &gt; yourself as speaking on behalf of The Labs unless explicitly &gt; authorized to do so.</p><p class=\"t-small\">e)  Follow any role-specific guidelines, editorial standards, &gt; facilitation protocols, or technical contribution practices &gt; communicated to you during onboarding or in writing by The Labs.</p><p class=\"t-small\">f)  Disclose any conflict of interest promptly (see Section 9).</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">4. Confidentiality</div><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">3.1 What is Confidential</div><p class=\"t-small\">In the course of your volunteer work, you may have access to non-public information about The Labs, its participants, partners, or operations. This includes personal data about participants, internal financial information, grant applications in progress, private cohort discussions, and technical infrastructure details (\"Confidential Information\").</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">3.2 Your Obligations</div><p class=\"t-small\">You agree to:</p><p class=\"t-small\">a)  use Confidential Information only for your volunteer activities &gt; within The Labs,</p><p class=\"t-small\">b)  not share it with anyone outside The Labs without written &gt; authorization, and</p><p class=\"t-small\">c)  take reasonable steps to keep it secure.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">3.3 What Is Not Confidential</div><p class=\"t-small\">Information that is publicly available, that you already knew independently, or that you receive from a third party without restriction is not Confidential Information. Much of what The Labs produces is intentionally public — open repositories, published blog posts, and event recaps are not confidential.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">3.4 Duration</div><p class=\"t-small\">Your confidentiality obligations continue for two (2) years after your volunteer participation ends. Your obligations with respect to personal data about participants continue for as long as you retain that data.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">5. Photos, Video, and Your Personal Data</div><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">4.1 Photo and Video</div><p class=\"t-small\">Your likeness may be captured in photos or videos at Labs events and used to promote the community and its programs. If you prefer not to be photographed or filmed, please let a staff member know before or at the event. You may also opt out at any time by notifying The Labs in writing. Any opt-out applies prospectively from the date we receive your request and does not require removal of materials already published or distributed.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">4.2 Your Information</div><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs collects and retains certain information about volunteers (such as name, email address, photo, and professional profile) to administer the volunteer program and promote the community. We will not sell your personal information to third parties. You may request access to, correction of, or deletion of your information by contacting us in writing. Upon the end of your participation, we will retain only what is necessary for legal, tax, or recordkeeping purposes.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">6. Intellectual Property and Contributions</div><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">5.1 Your Existing Work</div><p class=\"t-small\">You retain ownership of any work, frameworks, templates, or materials you created before joining The Labs or developed entirely outside your volunteer activities (\"Your Background IP\"). Nothing here transfers ownership of Your Background IP to The Labs.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">5.2 What You Contribute to Open Projects</div><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs produces open-source resources that are free for anyone to use, share, and adapt. If you contribute to these resources — whether written content, templates, workshop materials, slide decks, code, designs, or other materials — you acknowledge that:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Those contributions may be used, shared, modified, and built upon by others under the applicable open-source license for that project.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">You are representing that you have the right to contribute those materials and that doing so does not violate any obligations you have to a current or former employer or client.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">You will not later attempt to restrict use of materials you have already contributed to an open project.</li></ul><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">5.3 Sharing Without Contributing</div><p class=\"t-small\">If you share Your Background IP as a teaching example or reference without intending it to become a contribution, label it clearly as \"Not a Contribution / No License Granted.\" Avoid posting such materials in open project repositories or channels where reuse is expected.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">5.4 The Labs' Materials</div><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs' name, logos, trademarks, curricula, and internal documents remain the property of The Labs. You may use these only as needed to carry out your volunteer role and may not publish, sell, or distribute them outside The Labs without written permission.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">7. Personal Safety and Risk</div><p class=\"t-small\">Some volunteer activities — including in-person workshops, events, and meetups — involve inherent risks, including the possibility of personal injury, property damage, or illness. By participating, you acknowledge these risks and agree to exercise reasonable care for your own safety and the safety of others.</p><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs does not provide workers' compensation, health insurance, disability coverage, or general liability coverage for volunteers. We encourage you to confirm that your own personal insurance covers your volunteer activities before participating in any in-person event.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">8. Conflicts of Interest</div><p class=\"t-small\">Please disclose any material conflicts of interest promptly, including competitive relationships, financial interest in a participant's project, or intent to recruit participants into your own paid services or employment. Do not use The Labs primarily as a solicitation or recruiting channel. Reasonable professional networking is welcome; high-pressure sales or recruitment behavior is not.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">9. Publicity</div><p class=\"t-small\">You may be listed on The Labs' website and in our communications (name, role, title, organization, and photo) unless you opt out in writing. You may describe yourself as a volunteer at The Upskilling Labs on professional profiles such as LinkedIn. Please do not use The Labs' name or marks in a way that implies our organizational endorsement of your work or services without written permission.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">10. No Guarantees</div><p class=\"t-small\">Volunteer activities are educational and community-based in nature. Nothing in this Agreement constitutes legal, financial, medical, or other regulated professional services, unless explicitly agreed in writing and you are appropriately licensed to provide such services.</p><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs makes no guarantees regarding participant outcomes, employment, funding, project success, or the continued operation of any program or volunteer role.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">11. Limitation of Liability</div><p class=\"t-small\">To the maximum extent permitted by applicable law, The Labs will not be liable for indirect, incidental, special, or consequential damages arising from your volunteer participation or any Labs activities. The Labs' total liability to you under this Agreement will not exceed $100.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Similarly, your liability to The Labs arising from your volunteer activities — other than for intentional misconduct or fraud — will not exceed $100. Nothing here limits either party's responsibility for gross negligence, willful misconduct, or punitive damages.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">12. Updates to This Agreement</div><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs may update these terms from time to time to reflect changes in our programs, legal requirements, or community standards. When we do, we will notify you in writing. If you continue participating after receiving notice of an update, that constitutes your acceptance of the revised terms. If you do not accept a revision, you may end your participation by notifying us in writing.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">13. General</div><p class=\"t-small\">a)  This Agreement is governed by the laws of the District of Columbia, &gt; without regard to conflict-of-law rules.</p><p class=\"t-small\">b)  If any provision of this Agreement is found to be unenforceable, the &gt; remaining provisions stay in effect.</p><p class=\"t-small\">c)  This Agreement represents the full understanding between you and The &gt; Labs regarding your volunteer participation and supersedes any &gt; prior informal understandings on this subject.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\">Acknowledgment and Signature</div><p class=\"t-small\">By signing below, I confirm that I have read and understood this Volunteer Participation Agreement, that I agree to participate in accordance with its terms, and that I am signing voluntarily.</p><p class=\"t-small\">—————- -- <strong>Full Name:</strong> —————- --</p><p class=\"t-small\">———— -- <strong>Email:</strong> ———— --</p><p class=\"t-small\">—————- -- <strong>Signature:</strong> —————- --</p><p class=\"t-small\">———-- -- <strong>Date:</strong> ———-- --</p><p class=\"t-small\"><em>The Upskilling Labs, Inc.</em></p>"},
  guidelines: {"title":"The Volunteer and Participation Guidelines","version":"vg-2026-07-v1","html":"<p class=\"t-small\">The Upskilling Labs</p><p class=\"t-small\">Volunteer Guidelines</p><p class=\"t-small\">theupskillinglabs.org</p><p class=\"t-small\">Welcome to The Upskilling Labs volunteer community!</p><p class=\"t-small\">We're grateful you're here. Volunteers are not just support staff. At The Upskilling Labs, everything we do is made possible by volunteers. From mentoring project teams to managing events, building our digital infrastructure to telling our community's story, volunteers are the engine that keeps the Labs running and growing.</p><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs is an open, project-based learning community where people build real things with real people. Every quarter, we run a Build Cycle themed around an industry. Participants form pods around real problems, develop AI-enabled solutions together, and showcase what they've built. No credentials required. Just show up ready to learn alongside others. As a volunteer, you're not just supporting that mission. You're part of it.</p><p class=\"t-small\">YOUR ROLE</p><p class=\"t-small\">Volunteers support the Labs across seven teams. Each team has its own rhythm, responsibilities, and time commitment. You'll be matched based on your skills and interests. And within your team, you'll have clear responsibilities and a point of contact on the Labs organizing team.</p><p class=\"t-small\">We do not view any volunteer role as static. As the Labs grows, roles evolve. Bring your ideas, flag what's not working, and help us build something better.</p><p class=\"t-small\">PARTICIPANTS (UPSKILLERS), VOLUNTEER TEAMS &amp; ROLES</p><p class=\"t-small\">👷 <strong>Upskillers</strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">An Upskiller is a participant in The Upskilling Labs community — someone who shows up to learn, build, and contribute without waiting for credentials or permission.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Upskillers who join build cycles work in small research communities called Pods, tackle real problems using AI-enabled approaches, and form small project teams to build solutions. The role is peer-led and project-based: Upskillers aren't passive students — they're active builders navigating a period of massive technological change alongside others in the community. The term signals belonging and agency; it's the organization's way of saying you're already someone who does this work.</p><p class=\"t-small\">🧠 <strong>Mentors</strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">We don't view mentorship as a static title or a social hierarchy — it's an active practice. Mentors are expert peers: colleagues and community members who possess specialized knowledge and offer it to those navigating similar paths. That expertise comes in many forms: industry knowledge relevant to the current build cycle, technical depth (especially AI), and soft skills like design thinking, storytelling, and user research.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Mentors support the build cycles in two ways:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Workshop Facilitators design and deliver hands-on sessions across three tracks: soft skills (design thinking, storytelling, customer discovery), technology (prompt engineering, vibe coding, AI tools), and sector knowledge (healthcare AI, HIPAA, civic engagement, climate and energy, and other industry topics). Sessions can be held in-person or virtually.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Open Office Hours — scheduled sessions (virtual or in-person) — when a team hits a technical or tactical wall, the organizing team issues a call to mentors with relevant expertise. The mentor engages for a targeted 20-to-30-minute session to help the team think through the problem rather than solving it for them.</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\">Time commitment:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Lead workshops, <em>4 hours/quarter</em></li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Host Office Hours, <em>1 hour/week</em></li></ul><p class=\"t-small\">🏛️ <strong>Governance &amp; Grant Writing</strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">The Governance Team provides strategic oversight and ensures the organization stays true to its mission, makes sound decisions, and has the resources needed to grow. Grant Writing volunteers research funding opportunities and draft grant applications to support the Labs' programming, tools, and community growth.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Time commitment: <em>4--10 hours/week (varies by grant cycle)</em></p><p class=\"t-small\">💻 <strong>Research &amp; Development (Operating System)</strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">The R&amp;D Team builds and maintains the technical infrastructure that powers the Labs — from our operating system to the tools that enable seamless community engagement. Web &amp; Tools Development volunteers support the development and maintenance of our digital ecosystem, including the community tools, and any technology that supports program delivery.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Time commitment: <em>4--10 hours/week</em></p><p class=\"t-small\">⚙️ <strong>Programming &amp; Events</strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">The Programming Team organizes a variety of events including roundtables, workshops, and meetups. Two key roles:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Day-of Volunteers — support event logistics on the ground, handling registration, room setup, and attendee coordination to ensure everything runs smoothly.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Event Management Volunteers — help with venue coordination, scheduling, speaker logistics, and run-of-show planning in the weeks leading up to each event.</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\">Time commitment:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">4-6 hours/week (day of support)</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">72 hours/quarter (event management)</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\">📣 <strong>Communications and Marketing</strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">The Comms Team is the voice and visual identity of The Upskilling Labs. We're not just a marketing function — we handle branding, PR, philanthropic communications, and storytelling across all channels. We also support programming and engagement teams with internal communications, keeping participants informed and inspired every step of the way. Three focused roles:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Content Writing — craft compelling written content that brings the Labs' mission and community stories to life. This includes weekly newsletters (Mailchimp), blog posts and event recaps, workshop and meetup announcements, and brochures and partner communications.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Design — create visual assets that reinforce our brand identity across every touchpoint: event flyers, slide deck templates, branded social media graphics, and visual identity for campaigns.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Social Media — manage the Labs' social presence, schedule and publish LinkedIn posts, engage with followers, amplify event announcements, highlight member spotlights, and track engagement insights.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Digital fundraising - use online platforms, social media, email, and websites to collect donations, engage donors, and run campaigns.</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\">Time commitment: <em>4-10 hours/week per role</em></p><p class=\"t-small\">🤝 <strong>Participant Success (Pod Moderators)</strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">The Participant Success Team supports Upskillers throughout their journey, ensuring people are engaged, supported, and making progress toward their goals. Pod Moderators (Poderators) provide structure and support to teams during the build cycle. Their role is to maintain clarity of purpose, healthy working dynamics, and steady progress within a Pod — without directing the work, owning outcomes, or solving problems for participants.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Poderators encourage Upskillers to complete weekly check-in forms, communicate with other volunteers about participant needs and feedback, and serve as an early warning system when a team is struggling.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Time commitment: <em>4-10 hours/week via Slack and virtual meetings. Minimum of 4 volunteers needed per cycle.</em></p><p class=\"t-small\">THE DOS</p><p class=\"t-small\">Do show up consistently.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Reliability is the most important thing you can offer. If you've committed to a task, a shift, or a deadline, follow through. The rest of the team and the participants we serve are counting on you.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Do communicate early when something comes up.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Life happens. If you're going to miss a deadline, can't make an event, or need to step back temporarily, say so as soon as you know. A heads-up with time to adjust is always better than silence. Reach out in Slack or contact your team lead directly.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Do ask questions when you get stuck.</p><p class=\"t-small\">If you're unsure about a task, a deadline, or how something should be done — ask. Don't spend an hour guessing when a two-minute Slack message would get you unstuck. There are no dumb questions here, only missed opportunities to learn faster.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Do stay in your lane — and own it fully.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Each role has a defined scope for good reason. Know what you own, do it well, and resist the urge to take on more than you can handle. A volunteer who owns one lane and delivers consistently is more valuable than one who stretches across five and drops the ball.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Do stay on brand.</p><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs has a voice and a visual identity. When you're writing, posting, designing, or speaking on behalf of the organization, represent us consistently. When in doubt, refer to the brand guidelines or ask before publishing.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Do treat participants, staff, and fellow volunteers with respect.</p><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs is built on the belief that everyone has something to learn and something to contribute. Create an environment where people feel safe to show up, ask questions, and try new things. Be kind, be patient, and be encouraging.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Do celebrate the work.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Notice when something goes well — a workshop that lands, a post that resonates, a participant who finally figures something out. Say so. Positive feedback is fuel for a volunteer community.</p><p class=\"t-small\">THE DON'TS</p><p class=\"t-small\">Don't ghost team members.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Disappearing without notice is the most disruptive thing a volunteer can do. If tasks go unfinished and no one knows why, it creates extra work for everyone else and puts participants in a tough spot. If you're struggling, overloaded, or thinking about stepping back — tell us. We would rather adjust than lose you.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Don't post or publish without approval.</p><p class=\"t-small\">All external-facing content (social media posts, newsletters, event announcements, press outreach) needs to go through the review process before it goes live. This protects the organization and ensures our communications are consistent and accurate. When in doubt, draft and ask.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Don't make decisions on behalf of the organization.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Volunteers support and execute. They don't set strategy, make commitments to external partners, or speak officially on behalf of the Labs unless explicitly authorized. If someone asks you a question you're not sure about, say \"let me connect you with our team\" and loop in your lead.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Don't do the work for participants.</p><p class=\"t-small\">This applies especially to Mentors and Pod Moderators. No matter how much faster it would be, resist solving problems directly for participants. Ask guiding questions, point to resources, help them think, but let them do the work. That's how learning happens.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Don't work around the system.</p><p class=\"t-small\">All volunteer coordination happens through Slack and the Labs' shared tools. Don't set up separate channels, side conversations, or external workflows without talking to the team first. Keeping things visible and centralized is how we stay organized and make sure nothing falls through the cracks.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Don't lose patience.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Everyone at the Labs is at a different point in their journey. What feels obvious to you may be brand new to someone else. Lead with patience and meet people where they are. What's second nature to you now was once new to you too.</p><p class=\"t-small\">TOOLS AND COMMUNICATION</p><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs runs on a small, intentional tech stack. Here's what you'll use and how:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Slack: Primary communication platform. Check it regularly, respond within 24 hours on weekdays, and keep your status updated.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Google Workspace: Docs, Sheets, and Drive for shared documents, editorial calendars, and volunteer resources. Use the shared folders, don't create parallel systems.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Luma: Events and registration management. Volunteers supporting events will be given access to relevant event pages.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mailchimp: Used by the Comms team for newsletters and email communications.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Email: Used for formal communications and onboarding. For day-to-day coordination, Slack is preferred.</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\">YOUR COMMITMENT</p><p class=\"t-small\">Volunteer roles at the Labs are organized around our quarterly Build Cycles. At the start of each cycle, you'll confirm your availability and the team you're supporting. At the end of each cycle, you'll have the opportunity to renew, shift roles, or step back — no guilt, no awkward conversations.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Time commitments vary by role and are listed above. Be honest about your capacity when you sign up. Overcommitting and underdelivering is harder to recover from than setting realistic expectations upfront.</p><p class=\"t-small\">If your availability changes during a cycle, tell your team lead as early as possible. We'd rather adjust expectations mid-cycle than have work fall through the cracks.</p><p class=\"t-small\">WHEN IN DOUBT</p><p class=\"t-small\">Ask yourself: \"Am I making the Labs easier to run, or harder?\" That's the clearest signal for whether you're on the right track.</p><p class=\"t-small\">If you're unsure whether to post something, commit to something, or take action on something — pause and ask. The Labs team would always rather answer a question than clean up a misunderstanding.</p><p class=\"t-small\">And if something isn't working such as a process, a tool, or a role definition, say so. We're a learning community, and that means the way we run things should keep getting better too.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Thank you for being part of the Labs.</p><p class=\"t-small\">Your time, skills, and presence make the work possible. If you have questions about these guidelines</p><p class=\"t-small\">or need support in your volunteer role, reach out to the Labs team anytime.</p><p class=\"t-small\">theupskillinglabs.org</p>"},
  mentor: {"title":"The Volunteer Mentor Agreement","version":"vma-2026-07-v1","html":"<div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>The Upskilling Labs — Volunteer Mentor Agreement</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">This Volunteer Mentor Agreement (\"Agreement\") is entered into by and between <strong>The Upskilling Labs, Inc.</strong> (\"<strong>The Labs</strong>\") and the undersigned volunteer mentor (\"<strong>Mentor</strong>\"), effective as of the date of signature (\"<strong>Effective Date</strong>\").</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>1) Purpose</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs runs peer-driven learning cycles and open, public-facing projects. Mentor will volunteer time to support participants (\"Participants\") through guidance, feedback, introductions, and technical or strategic mentorship.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>2) Volunteer Status (No Compensation)</strong></div><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mentor serves <strong>voluntarily</strong> and is <strong>not</strong> an employee, contractor, partner, agent, or joint venturer of The Labs.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mentor will <strong>not</strong> receive compensation or benefits.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Any expense reimbursement must be <strong>pre-approved in writing</strong> by The Labs.</li></ul><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>3) Term and Termination</strong></div><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">This Agreement continues until terminated.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Either party may terminate this Agreement <strong>at any time</strong>, with or without cause, by written notice.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Sections intended to survive (including IP, confidentiality, disclaimers, and liability) will survive termination.</li></ul><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>4) Mentor Role and Conduct</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">Mentor agrees to:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Provide guidance in line with our [[Mentor Guidelines]{.underline}](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Mbj81L_7MqTvUE0in63Q3lDKg7Yxqac0/edit#heading=h.aqkv7hmugt4l), in a manner consistent with The Labs' community standards and any applicable code of conduct.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Avoid harassment, discrimination, or retaliation.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Not directly sell their services or other paid offerings through the Upskilling Labs community and events without written consent from The Labs. Reasonable professional networking is allowed; high-pressure sales behavior is not.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Use reasonable care when providing advice and avoid representing Mentor as speaking on behalf of The Labs unless explicitly authorized</li></ul><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>5) Confidentiality and Privacy</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>5.1 Confidential Information.</strong> \"Confidential Information\" means non-public information The Labs or Participants share with Mentor that is marked or reasonably understood to be confidential (e.g., personal data, internal operations, private cohort discussions, non-public partner info).</p><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>5.2 Exclusions.</strong> Confidential Information does not include information that:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">is publicly available without breach,</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">was already known to Mentor without obligation,</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">is independently developed without use of Confidential Information, or</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">is lawfully obtained from a third party.</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>5.3 Obligations.</strong> Mentor will:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">use Confidential Information only for mentoring within The Labs,</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">not disclose it to third parties except as authorized in writing, and</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">take reasonable steps to protect it.</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>5.4 Open Work is Not Confidential.</strong> Mentor understands that many Labs activities are conducted in public (e.g., open repos, public demos). Information intentionally posted to open channels is <strong>not</strong> Confidential Information.</p><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>5.5 Photo and video release.</strong> You agree that your likeness may be captured via photo and video at events associated with The Upskilling Labs, and be used by The Upskilling Labs to help promote the community and event.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>6) Intellectual Property (IP) — Mentor Pre-Existing IP Protected</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>6.1 Mentor Retains Ownership.</strong> Mentor retains all right, title, and interest in:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">any inventions, templates, frameworks, code, materials, trademarks, know-how, or content created <strong>before</strong> this Agreement or developed <strong>outside</strong> Labs activities (\"Mentor Background IP\").</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>6.2 No Transfer by Default.</strong> Except for the open-source license grant in Section 7, nothing in this Agreement transfers ownership of Mentor Background IP to The Labs or Participants.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>7) Open Source Contributions — License Grant for What Mentor Introduces</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">The Labs Participants may work on projects intended to be <strong>open source</strong> (\"Open Projects\").</p><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>7.1 Project Licenses Control.</strong> Each Open Project may be governed by an applicable open-source license (e.g., MIT, Apache-2.0, GPL) and/or contribution terms posted in the project repository (\"Project License\").</p><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>7.2 Mentor Contribution = Permission to Use.</strong> If Mentor <strong>contributes</strong> to an Open Project—whether by code, documentation, designs, datasets, prompts, playbooks, or other materials (\"Contributions\")—Mentor agrees that:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mentor is <strong>granting a license</strong> (or sublicensable rights as required) for those Contributions to be used, modified, distributed, and otherwise exploited under the <strong>Project License</strong>, and</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mentor will not later attempt to revoke those rights for Contributions already made available to the Open Project.</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>7.3 You Only Contribute What You Can License.</strong> Mentor represents that for any Contribution:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mentor either owns it or has sufficient rights to grant the license described above, and</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mentor will not knowingly contribute materials that violate third-party rights (including employer/contractor IP obligations).</li></ul><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>7.4 Optional: Marking Background IP.</strong> If Mentor shares Mentor Background IP as an example or teaching aid <strong>without</strong> intending it to become a Contribution, Mentor should clearly label it \"<strong>Not a Contribution / No License Granted</strong>.\" The Labs will make reasonable efforts to respect such labeling; however, Mentor should avoid posting such materials into Open Project repositories or channels where reuse is expected.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>8) The Labs IP and Materials</strong></div><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">The Labs retains ownership of its name, logos, trademarks, curricula, and internal documents (\"Labs Materials\").</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mentor receives a limited, revocable, non-exclusive license to use Labs Materials <strong>only</strong> as needed to perform mentoring. Mentor may not publish, sell, or distribute Labs Materials outside The Labs without written permission.</li></ul><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>9) Publicity</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">Mentor may be listed publicly as a mentor (name, title, organization, and photo) unless Mentor opts out in writing. Mentor may list themselves as a \"Mentor\" at \"The Upskilling Labs\" on LinkedIn. Mentor may not use The Labs' name or marks in a way that implies endorsement without written permission.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>10) Conflicts of Interest</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">Mentor will disclose any material conflicts (e.g., mentoring a direct competitor relationship, or recruiting Participants into paid services) and agrees not to use The Labs primarily as a solicitation channel. Reasonable professional networking is allowed; high-pressure sales behavior is not.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>11) Disclaimers</strong></div><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">Mentorship is educational and community-based. Mentor is not providing legal, medical, financial, or other regulated professional services through The Labs unless explicitly agreed in writing and appropriately licensed.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">The Labs makes no guarantees regarding outcomes, employment, funding, or project success.</li></ul><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>12) Limitation of Liability</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">To the maximum extent permitted by law:</p><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">The Labs will not be liable for indirect, incidental, special, consequential, or punitive damages arising out of the mentoring relationship.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">The Labs' total liability under this Agreement will not exceed <strong>$100</strong>.</li></ul><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>13) Indemnity (Basic)</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">Mentor agrees to indemnify and hold harmless The Labs from third-party claims arising from Mentor's intentional misconduct or knowing infringement of third-party IP rights through Mentor Contributions.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>14) Governing Law</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\">This Agreement is governed by the laws of the <strong>District of Columbia</strong>, without regard to conflict-of-law rules.</p><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>15) Miscellaneous</strong></div><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">This Agreement is the entire agreement on this subject and supersedes prior discussions.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">If any provision is unenforceable, the rest remains in effect.</li></ul><ul class=\"agree-ul\"><li class=\"t-small\">This Agreement may be signed electronically and in counterparts.</li></ul><div class=\"t-h4 agree-h\"><strong>Signatures</strong></div><p class=\"t-small\"><strong>MENTOR (Volunteer)\\ </strong></p><p class=\"t-small\">Name: _____________________________</p><p class=\"t-small\">Email: _____________________________</p><p class=\"t-small\">Signature: _________________________</p><p class=\"t-small\">Date: ______________________________</p>"}
};
